## Feature #2 Increment 3 — `collect` PARKS on a reactor-backed child instead
## of busy-spinning (Codex high finding). A reactor-backed child (a
## `TimeSleepFuture` from `Async.sleep`) registers a timer and returns
## Pending; further pumps make no progress until the timer fires.
## `collect` must PARK (`Thread.yield_now` → reactor wait) when a pump makes
## no progress and no task is ready — otherwise it busy-spins the CPU.
##
## We spawn a ~30ms `Async.sleep` future directly (its Output is nil → the
## scheduler stores 0, so `collect` sees status 0). The test completing
## within the harness wall-clock cap proves `collect` drives the timer child
## by parking on the reactor between pumps, not spinning.

use std.time.Duration

def main
  puts "scope start"
  Scope.open do |s|
    s.spawn(Async.sleep(Duration.from_millis(30)))
    let status = s.collect
    puts "collect status=#{status}"
  end
  puts "scope done"
end
